# Translation maintenance

- Every change to website/app copy includes English, Arabic and Russian translations
  alongside Hebrew. This includes recovery messages, connector guidance and static API labels.
- Run `npm --prefix web run i18n:sync` after changing copy, then `npm --prefix web run i18n:check`.
  Sync reuses reviewed messages and translates only new/changed source messages.
- Keep canonical source copy in code or a source registry when wiring message IDs;
  moving a label to a translation call must not remove its original source definition.
- Install the repository commit hook in a new checkout with
  `python3 scripts/install-translation-hook.py`. CI independently checks source coverage.
- Keep numbers, ranges, missing-data states, permissions and action meanings faithful.
  Approving a hypothesis does not prove it; approving a post does not publish it.
- Review natural wording and ambiguity in context. Machine checks alone are not a native
  language or legal review. Keep source references and review notes with catalog changes.
- Interface language is independent of business content language. Never rewrite stored
  customer content, names, answers or evidence, or trigger paid generation on page reads.
- Do not change enum/API identifiers, callbacks, tracked links or budgets through translation.
- Shahar now requests the visible language picker. Clearly identify unfinished translation
  coverage until whole journeys are translated and reviewed; preserve drafts on switching.
- Customer-facing releases include translated, dated notes in `web/lib/releases.ts`.
